<p>Not every lead is a good fit, and that’s fine. Finding out early means you spend your time on people you can really help, and tell the others kindly and quickly.</p>

<h2>Four things to know</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Need</strong>: do they have a problem you solve well?</li>
  <li><strong>Timing</strong>: is there a reason to act soon, or is this “one day”?</li>
  <li><strong>Decision</strong>: are you talking to the person who decides, or who will they ask?</li>
  <li><strong>Budget</strong>: is what they can spend in the range of what it costs?</li>
</ul>
<p>You don’t need all four on the first call. When you’re confident about most of them, move the lead to <em>Qualified</em>: that’s the signal to put real time into a proposal.</p>

<h2>Asking about money without awkwardness</h2>
<p><em>“Projects like this usually range from … to …, depending on …. Does that fit what you had in mind?”</em> Giving a range first makes it easy for them to answer honestly.</p>

<h2>When it’s not a fit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Be direct and warm: <em>“From what you’ve said, I don’t think we’re the best fit, and here’s why….”</em></li>
  <li>Suggest an alternative if you know one.</li>
  <li>Mark the lead <em>Lost</em> with a note. If it’s junk, mark it as spam instead.</li>
</ul>
